WEBVTT

NOTE automatic transcription, uncorrected

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.045
I think the most interesting question about Cedric Price would be something about

00:00:06.045 --> 00:00:12.015
how he still, how is Cedric still a part of architectural culture today, in your

00:00:12.015 --> 00:00:15.000
mind. That would be a possible question.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.412
and in my view Cedric is a kind of figure that haunts architecture in its

00:00:22.412 --> 00:00:24.240
contemporary life.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:29.379
Cedric imagined architecture in the 21st century being something I think

00:00:29.379 --> 00:00:31.020
fundamentally different

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:36.655
than what it's become. I think his career, his principles, his instinct was to see

00:00:36.655 --> 00:00:37.480
architecture

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:43.674
as a culture of ideas and almost at times thought experiments about what the world

00:00:43.674 --> 00:00:44.580
could be and

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:52.840
and that part of architecture's job was to ask questions about the world we know.

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:58.260
I think in that way he haunts architecture and what it's become

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:03.240
because of architecture's increasing and rampant professionalization,

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:09.920
commercial reality, and what I at times call almost industrial condition today.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:14.626
Architecture is industrializing in at times what I would say are almost horrific

00:01:14.626 --> 00:01:14.920
ways.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:18.920
It's getting better at doing more and thinking less.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:23.206
In other words, architecture is becoming less like Cedric than most of us could have

00:01:23.206 --> 00:01:23.920
ever imagined.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:28.265
My immediate impression, which I'll hold on to forever, is that he was an

00:01:28.265 --> 00:01:28.920
Englishman.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:34.920
And it had two distinct properties, let's say, for a crude American like me.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:42.094
like me. One is Cedric's immediate wit and playfulness, which I think people have

00:01:42.094 --> 00:01:42.360
too

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:51.860
often emphasized as the primary quality of his personality. He was a very witty guy.

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:58.806
On the surface, appeared to be very jokey and playful, but of course what he was

00:01:58.806 --> 00:01:59.240
doing

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:04.297
with the kind of jokes he would make and the experiments and games that he would

00:02:04.297 --> 00:02:04.740
play is

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:08.910
really interrogate the habits and the assumptions of architects. It was actually

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:09.640
deadly, deadly

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:14.796
serious work, but presented in the most English, low-key English of manners, I

00:02:14.796 --> 00:02:15.920
think. Incredibly

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:22.369
brilliant kind of critical method. The playfulness was probably the most distinctive

00:02:22.369 --> 00:02:23.060
property,

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:29.324
but the one is, at least as I got to know the body of work, which to this day I

00:02:29.324 --> 00:02:29.720
still

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:36.268
think about. The flip side of that, Englishness, is in fact a deeply London

00:02:36.268 --> 00:02:37.840
sensibility, which

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:44.017
is to see architecture in its global culture and to know exactly where modern

00:02:44.017 --> 00:02:44.980
architecture

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:52.870
is at any one time in his life. And I don't know where that ability came from, but I

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:53.340
think

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:59.616
a huge part of that ability resulted from him being at a place that was deeply

00:02:59.616 --> 00:03:00.340
connected

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:05.187
to what architecture was doing at all times. London, remember, is an incredibly

00:03:05.187 --> 00:03:05.520
unique

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:11.240
architectural culture. It's very worldly and has been for decades. One of my

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:12.520
absolute favorite

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:18.248
pictures I have of events at the AA, which I've used in lectures around the world

00:03:18.248 --> 00:03:18.800
forever,

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:26.800
This is a photograph taken in the late 1950s down in what now is our lecture hall,

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:32.800
and what at the time would have been a kind of member space for the association.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:37.800
And in the middle of the long wall facing the square is an old,

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:42.800
and it looks to me like very grouchy Walter Gropius presenting a lecture

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:47.800
to the crowd of ladies and gentlemen at a series of dinner tables.

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:51.545
And it's a very beautiful picture, and Gropius has got his notes and his reading

00:03:51.545 --> 00:03:51.920
glasses,

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:55.100
and he's making some pronouncement about what modern architecture is.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:59.752
And in the foreground of that picture, sitting sort of sideways and looking down at

00:03:59.752 --> 00:04:00.300
the floor,

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:05.180
is a young Cedric Price, who is the only person in the room not looking at Gropius.

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:10.531
And what Cedric is doing is sort of staring off into space and clearly processing

00:04:10.531 --> 00:04:11.880
what Gropius is saying,

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:14.380
but in no way following the great master

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:19.480
in a way that every other person in that room is clearly doing.

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:23.620
Cedric is already, and this is only within a couple of years of his graduation,

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:26.180
thinking about architecture in different directions,

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:28.960
and I think it's quite an amazing kind of record of that capacity

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:31.300
from the very beginning of his career.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:35.540
Again, I think the primary means for him to be able to do that

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:37.480
isn't just a kind of personal intelligence,

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:43.518
but a fact that he was being exposed to a range of ideas in modern architectural

00:04:43.518 --> 00:04:45.480
culture here in the 1950s,

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:51.952
which is already a city that's globalizing and international in ways that few other

00:04:51.952 --> 00:04:52.760
places were.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:58.277
It's not unprecedented that some careers have influence because of activities other

00:04:58.277 --> 00:04:59.140
than building.

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:02.440
Cedric is an extreme example of that.

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:06.940
I suppose the immediate answer many people would suggest

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:09.920
is that the ideas were of the kind of importance

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:12.940
that they gathered other minds around them that then did work

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:16.300
and we could then see influence being exerted

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:19.140
and that's a well-rehearsed genealogy

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:20.560
that I don't need to repeat here

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:25.140
Cedric's ideas become the basis for a kind of high-tech architecture

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:28.600
which is then commercialized, built by a generation

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:29.620
that comes after him

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:34.900
But I think the other reason that influence can be tracked in his career,

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:38.140
which I absolutely would subscribe to and believe in,

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:43.260
is because what we think of as influence is possible in that form

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:45.900
in that period of the 20th century especially,

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:49.540
i.e. at a moment when there wasn't a huge amount of building,

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:53.420
as things start to settle down after the rush of post-war building in England.

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:58.020
In fact, architectural culture during the 1960s and 70s

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:01.460
is really a world of not building.

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:05.040
I mean, terrifically so, you could say, almost.

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:07.700
That after the great boom of the 1950s,

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:11.580
when post-war England and much of Europe

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:13.820
has to rebuild itself after its destruction,

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:17.760
that activity begins to settle by the late 1950s

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:20.780
at exactly the moment Cedric's career starts to emerge.

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:26.580
And architecture becomes, you know,

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:29.500
and the AA is a tremendous record of this,

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:34.240
architecture becomes a discussion of ideas and principles

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:38.840
and possible projects, which of course we then see realized,

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:42.900
but only after economic and other activity starts to accelerate.

00:06:43.420 --> 00:06:50.140
So, you know, Cedric's capacity to distill that possibility

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:53.360
happens at exactly the moment when that becomes a dominant mode

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:56.200
of transmitting what you and I would think of as influence.

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:59.863
The very opposite sort of condition is the case today, where basically everybody is

00:06:59.863 --> 00:07:00.260
building,

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:05.247
and us trying to imagine how influence could be asserted by a set of principles or

00:07:05.247 --> 00:07:07.000
ideas becomes nearly inconceivable.

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:09.460
In fact, it becomes a really interesting project again today,

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:14.360
and I think why us talking about Cedric would be so important, let's say, in 2014.

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:19.660
I think an interesting question is, what might be Cedric's most important project?

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:24.120
and my response would be his office,

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:26.220
the creating of his office

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:28.620
and not the specific designs

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:31.760
of any of his 160 design proposals.

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:35.020
If I'm right, the number is something like

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:37.320
150 or 160 projects he does

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:39.800
over the course of his four or five decades of work.

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:42.640
Everybody on the planet today

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:44.720
who loves the fun palace to death

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:46.340
wants to say there are some projects

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:47.540
that matter more than others.

00:07:47.540 --> 00:07:52.840
I worry about a moment like ours where everybody can be so universally

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:56.840
agreed that Cedric's Fun Palace is the most important building of the

00:07:56.840 --> 00:08:00.440
20th century. It just seems weird to me that we could all suddenly agree on that.

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:04.741
I tell you, I can go to juries all week long at the A and everybody will do nothing

00:08:04.741 --> 00:08:04.880
but

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:08.840
endless praise on Fun Palace. I think that would terrify him as a kind of

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:12.020
generational condition.

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:16.780
I would, just to offer a slightly different view on that question,

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:19.720
and believe me, I love the Fun Palace for all the reasons everybody else does,

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:25.160
but I think actually the way he designs his office

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:26.720
becomes incredibly important

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:30.700
because he creates a model for an office that is a repository of ideas

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:34.480
much more than a factory for producing buildings.

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:40.040
And this I know because of the work that Samantha Hardingham

00:08:40.040 --> 00:08:42.000
and other colleagues have done

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:46.300
on the archival organization of his office,

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:49.640
which he saw as one of his many design projects.

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:52.320
And of course what you can then realize looking back

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:55.740
is that Cedric understood all along the organization of knowledge,

00:08:55.820 --> 00:08:57.660
even his own experience as an architect,

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:02.900
is something to be conscious of and articulate in his work as an architect.

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:06.800
Very, very few architects have that degree of self-awareness.

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:11.040
Most will see that as just the site where they do their serious work,

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:14.220
and the serious work will always be those buildings or proposals

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:18.160
or Cedric, for whatever reason,

00:09:18.260 --> 00:09:20.720
had a level of self-consciousness and self-awareness

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:22.440
that allowed him to see that the creation

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:26.660
of that very special space of all architectural spaces,

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:28.100
which is the design studio,

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:31.540
needs to be brought on board as how you think about your career.

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:34.640
And he did that to an almost obsessive level

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:39.520
of cataloging and organizing and sorting drawings,

00:09:39.820 --> 00:09:43.220
notes, sketches, postcards, newspaper clippings,

00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:47.931
clippings into something that you and I would today think of as almost a kind of

00:09:47.931 --> 00:09:48.520
curatorial

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:54.661
obsession. And I find that one of the least remarked upon qualities of his career,

00:09:54.661 --> 00:09:55.260
which is

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:58.794
just, you know, the conversations we've had about the role of the archive today, an

00:09:58.794 --> 00:09:59.220
incredibly

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:02.997
interesting model, let's say, for what architects might think about for the future.

00:10:02.997 --> 00:10:03.680
I think it's an

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:08.707
interesting question how Cedric comes to an idea that he's often quoted for, which

00:10:08.707 --> 00:10:09.320
is that an

00:10:09.320 --> 00:10:14.987
architect is an enabler and not a builder. And what he points to is what by the

00:10:14.987 --> 00:10:15.920
middle of the

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:20.201
20th century is a professional reality, which is that architects become largely

00:10:20.201 --> 00:10:20.960
organizers and

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:26.290
managers of the flow of information and ideas around, for example, the building of

00:10:26.290 --> 00:10:26.940
buildings,

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:32.996
but also many other activities. And it's been said by other people in the same years

00:10:32.996 --> 00:10:33.280
that

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:37.180
Cedric is a part of the AA school, but that the

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:40.360
architect has a very unique position as a profession

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:44.340
of sitting in the center of all kinds of forms of expertise, and

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:48.460
he or she is the figure that has to manage that, that flow of information.

00:10:49.460 --> 00:10:52.480
But the other thing he's doing, let's say, more

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:58.380
ideologically is saying, look, architecture is not equal to building.

00:10:58.880 --> 00:11:01.800
Those two things are two very different worlds.

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:05.800
Buildings are important and they often play a role within architectural culture,

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:09.800
but architectural culture is really a world of ideas.

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:15.800
And ideas can be expressed and discovered and developed in many different forms

00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:17.800
and not just actual building.

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:20.800
The making of drawings, the representing of how we move through space

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:22.800
and all of those kind of activities.

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:27.970
Rem once said that Price's intent was to make architecture indistinguishable from

00:11:27.970 --> 00:11:28.800
the ordinary,

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:33.766
i.e. that architecture isn't separate from the everyday lives and spaces of the

00:11:33.766 --> 00:11:34.080
city.

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:38.200
I think that's Rem's projection much more than Cedric's ambition.

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:42.820
I think that might be the case that Cedric wanted to live that way very much

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:47.566
and wanted to be very much indistinguishable from the wonderful world that he lived

00:11:47.566 --> 00:11:47.680
in

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:50.540
and was a part of and much larger than architectural culture.

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:54.668
But that's a very different thing than trying to make the case that that's what

00:11:54.668 --> 00:11:55.860
architecture should become.

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:59.960
In fact, I think one of Cedric's most interesting qualities

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:03.340
is that he shied away from the instinct

00:12:03.340 --> 00:12:05.820
that Kuhlhaus and many others embodies today,

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:10.280
which is to see architecture as a set of large-scale principles

00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:12.620
that apply across very wide audiences

00:12:12.620 --> 00:12:14.320
and not just to individual careers.

00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:17.580
I think part of the brilliance of Cedric's career

00:12:17.580 --> 00:12:21.400
is that it was always thought of in very intimate and very personal terms.

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:24.400
In fact, almost in ways in which it would be very hard

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:27.640
for you and I to even apply some of what he does to our own lives.

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:32.200
Which is a very, very different model than you could say the kind of classical

00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:36.240
modernism of Kuhlhaus' stance, which is to say, what I do

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:40.300
becomes a model for what architecture should become. Very interesting and

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:44.100
complex debate there, and that sort of thing. Maybe that's the one lesson of

00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:44.500
Cedric's

00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:47.500
that isn't often taken on board by his otherwise deeply

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:51.760
convinced followers of what he was arguing for.

00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:55.878
It's an interesting question many people pose, which is what might possibly be

00:12:55.878 --> 00:12:58.360
Cedric's relevance to architecture in our time.

00:12:59.020 --> 00:13:03.469
And I would say to learn from it from a distance, to not try and embody that

00:13:03.469 --> 00:13:05.460
project, to not try and extend it.

00:13:06.660 --> 00:13:13.543
I think, for me, one of the most unnerving questions of Cedric's career now is how

00:13:13.543 --> 00:13:17.320
and why does it live in the way that it does,

00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:25.319
i.e. largely forgotten and literally buried in the cold ground below Montreal in the

00:13:25.319 --> 00:13:25.700
CCA,

00:13:26.200 --> 00:13:29.100
which I find at times shocking.

00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:36.080
It's such an important body of work and is already now so distant from where

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:37.260
architecture is

00:13:37.260 --> 00:13:42.800
that I think like many people, and I know you are in the making of a film like this,

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:46.200
our speculation on how to bring those ideas back

00:13:46.200 --> 00:13:48.960
is in fact an incredibly important project.

00:13:49.460 --> 00:13:51.560
I don't have an answer to how to do that now.

00:13:51.680 --> 00:13:55.240
I think at a personal level, for example, as a publisher,

00:13:56.140 --> 00:14:02.200
I couldn't be more happy than to have helped people like yourselves,

00:14:02.200 --> 00:14:05.080
not just with this project, but with the brilliant work you guys did

00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:08.860
a couple of years ago, gathering Cedric's writings together.

00:14:08.860 --> 00:14:15.660
I think Samantha's two-volume definitive career monograph

00:14:15.660 --> 00:14:19.980
on a hidden career that will come out at the end of this year

00:14:19.980 --> 00:14:23.260
and will document 50 years of architectural discovery

00:14:23.260 --> 00:14:25.980
will be the book of our time.

00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:29.180
There will be no more important book in our time published.

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:34.500
Not just because it will be able to reveal the scope of a career

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:36.280
that very few people actually know,

00:14:36.280 --> 00:14:37.800
other than through one or two images,

00:14:37.800 --> 00:14:43.300
but also to see its true breadth from lectures, performances, installations,

00:14:43.620 --> 00:14:46.360
to the design of buildings, to the making of an office,

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:50.360
to a teaching career, to his activities as a critic.

00:14:51.040 --> 00:14:52.280
All of that will be important.

00:14:53.340 --> 00:14:55.880
But those are just, I think, the beginnings of the ways

00:14:55.880 --> 00:14:58.020
in which we really tease out the bigger questions,

00:14:58.020 --> 00:15:00.540
which is how can one make careers like that

00:15:00.540 --> 00:15:04.760
for a generation of architects emerging today alive again

00:15:04.760 --> 00:15:08.480
and available for their reinvention and rediscovery

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:10.520
and appropriation and use.

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:14.380
And I think Cedric would always say misuse by those who come after.

00:15:14.620 --> 00:15:18.180
I think the last thing he would want of us is that we worship him.

00:15:19.020 --> 00:15:20.840
I think he went out of his way, in fact,

00:15:20.900 --> 00:15:22.400
to make sure that would never happen.

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:26.780
And I think we have to remember he was doing that at a time

00:15:26.780 --> 00:15:29.240
in which the architect as a kind of cult figure,

00:15:30.280 --> 00:15:33.320
designer, celebrity, proprietary operating system

00:15:33.320 --> 00:15:35.880
was emerging in the latter decades of the 20th century,

00:15:36.360 --> 00:15:38.260
he did everything he could do

00:15:38.260 --> 00:15:41.000
to make sure he would never be that kind of a figure.

00:15:42.400 --> 00:15:44.240
Again, likely just instinct

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:47.120
and a very good, light, magic touch

00:15:47.120 --> 00:15:49.460
in terms of how to live in the world properly.

00:15:50.360 --> 00:15:52.980
But I think he's seen the horrors

00:15:52.980 --> 00:15:54.600
of what architecture was becoming,

00:15:54.820 --> 00:15:56.720
however brilliant its propositions were

00:15:56.720 --> 00:15:58.120
in the early 20th century.

00:15:59.300 --> 00:16:01.160
In the closing decades of the 20th century,

00:16:01.280 --> 00:16:03.080
he's seen professional activity,

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:07.020
architectural education, architectural journalism,

00:16:07.020 --> 00:16:10.100
all of these things literally industrializing

00:16:10.100 --> 00:16:12.660
into big, large-scale commercial activities

00:16:12.660 --> 00:16:15.980
that weren't really offering more and more ideas

00:16:15.980 --> 00:16:18.400
for the world to speculate on and think about,

00:16:18.400 --> 00:16:20.260
but just simply keeping themselves alive

00:16:20.260 --> 00:16:21.740
as little self-sustaining things.

00:16:21.740 --> 00:16:24.480
And I think what he really asks us all to do

00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:28.280
is to stand back from that activity periodically

00:16:28.280 --> 00:16:30.320
and to think again for the ways in which,

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:33.240
even at the most individual, personal level,

00:16:33.240 --> 00:16:38.960
we can offer or speculate upon alternatives to that kind of reality.

00:16:39.740 --> 00:16:41.700
And I think that's the one thing he would want us to do today

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:45.400
and we should all try and do for Cedric, if not for ourselves.
